From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hiroyuki Ikegami <ikegam@mixallow.net>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 20:34:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: dwc: gs201-cp: don't let resume wait for the modem's
 link

PCI_DEV_FLAGS_NO_D3 kept the root port out of D3hot, but it can still be
marked D3cold. The read that does it is pci_power_up()'s read of the
port's PM control register: with the link down, cp_root_map_bus() maps
nothing, the read returns all ones, and pci_power_up() marks the port
D3cold:

  pcieport 0001:00:00.0: Unable to change power state from D3cold to D0, device inaccessible

The suspend-to-idle after that one started from D3cold, so resume again
waited for the link, which only the modem driver raises, and marked the
modem disconnected:

  pcieport 0001:00:00.0: Data Link Layer Link Active not set in 100 msec
  cpif: s51xx_pcie_restore_state: Can't set D0 state!!!!
  cpif: s51xx_pcie_send_doorbell_int: BME is not set(cnt=10)

Give the devices below the port a zero d3cold_delay, so
pci_bridge_wait_for_secondary_bus() returns at once and the link stays
the modem driver's business, as at runtime. This makes the stale D3cold
mark harmless rather than avoiding it, since the modem's power belongs
to CPIF. Skipping the wait is safe because the modem is never powered
down across s2idle, and the modem driver waits for its own link up and
restores the endpoint's state before it touches the modem. The wait
could only time out and mark a working modem disconnected, so the zero
d3cold_delay must stay.

Only with sleep_guard (gs201): the Pixel 9a's devices keep their
d3cold_delay.

With this and the two changes before it, on a Pixel 7 Pro with the modem
registered on LTE: eight suspend-to-idle cycles in one boot, all resumed,
woken by the RTC alarm or the modem's cp2ap_wakeup (an incoming SMS among
them, received in the second it woke), mobile data working after each,
no UFS error.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Hiroyuki Ikegami <ikegam@mixallow.net>
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
index de7923787..a730f543c 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
@@ -762,6 +762,12 @@ static int cp_msi_setup(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int cp_no_d3cold_wait(struct pci_dev *pdev, void *data)
+{
+	pdev->d3cold_delay = 0;
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int cp_bus_report(struct pci_dev *pdev, void *data)
 {
 	struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp = data;
@@ -834,12 +840,25 @@ static int cp_bus(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
 	 * left to the PCI core, a system sleep with the link down marked the
 	 * root port D3cold, and resume then took the modem as disconnected,
 	 * since the link only comes back when the modem driver asks for it.
-	 * Only where system sleep with the modem up has been tried
-	 * (sleep_guard, gs201).
+	 * PCI_DEV_FLAGS_NO_D3 keeps it in D0, but a read of its PM control
+	 * register with the link down (pci_power_up() at resume, through
+	 * cp_root_map_bus(), which maps nothing then, so the read is all
+	 * ones) still marks it D3cold, and the next resume would wait up to
+	 * d3cold_delay for a link only the modem driver raises, then mark the
+	 * modem disconnected. The modem keeps its power across s2idle, and
+	 * its driver waits for its own link up and restores the endpoint
+	 * before any access, so that wait has nothing to wait for: keep
+	 * d3cold_delay zero below the port. Both only where system sleep with
+	 * the modem up has been tried (sleep_guard, gs201).
 	 */
-	if (cp->data->sleep_guard)
-		list_for_each_entry(rp, &cp->bridge->bus->devices, bus_list)
+	if (cp->data->sleep_guard) {
+		list_for_each_entry(rp, &cp->bridge->bus->devices, bus_list) {
 			rp->dev_flags |= PCI_DEV_FLAGS_NO_D3;
+			if (rp->subordinate)
+				pci_walk_bus(rp->subordinate,
+					     cp_no_d3cold_wait, NULL);
+		}
+	}
 	pci_walk_bus(cp->bridge->bus, cp_bus_report, cp);
 	cp_root_win_save(cp);
 
